{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>משתאה.</b> A term of שְׁאִיָּה, as in ״שָׁאוּ עָרִים״, ״תִּשָּׁאֶה שְׁמָמָה״ (ibid. 6)." "<b>משתאה.</b> Astonished and bewildered because he saw his matter close to succeeding, but he did not know whether she was from Avraham's family or not. And do not wonder at the tav of מִשְׁתָּאֵה — for there is no word whose root foundation begins with shin and speaks in the hitpa'el binyan where tav separates between the two root letters, such as מִשְׁתָּאֵה, from the root שׁוֹלָל; וַיִּשְׁתּוֹמֵם from the root שְׁמָמָה; וְיִשְׁתַּמֵּר חֻקּוֹת עָמְרִי (Micah 6) from וַיִּשְׁמֹר. So too here, מִשְׁתָּאֵה is from the root תִּשָּׁאֶה. And just as you find the term מְשׁוֹמֵם in a person who is alarmed and struck silent and full of thought — as in ״עַל יוֹמוֹ נָשַׁמּוּ אַחֲרֹנִים״ (Job 18), ״שֹׁמּוּ שָׁמַיִם״ (Jeremiah 2), ״אֶשְׁתּוֹמַם כְּשָׁעָה חֲדָא״ (Daniel 4) — so too you may explain the term שְׁאִיָּה in a person who is bewildered and full of thought. (And Onkelos translated it as a term of שְׁהִיָּה — וְגַבְרָא שָׁהֵי — staring and standing in one place to see whether HaShem would make his way prosper. And one should not translate it as שָׁתֵי, for it is not a term of drinking — an alef does not drop out in the term שְׁתִיָּה.)" "<b>משתאה לה.</b> Astonished at her — like ״אִמְרִי לִי אָחִי הוּא״ (Genesis 20:13), and like ״וַיִּשְׁאֲלוּ אַנְשֵׁי הַמָּקוֹם לְאִשְׁתּוֹ״ (ibid. 26)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {1 "Rashi's cross-reference 'ibid. 6' in comment 0 points to Isaiah 6, the same biblical book as the first citation (שאו ערים, Isaiah 6:11); תשאה שממה is Isaiah 5:9 or 6:11 depending on edition.", 2 "Rashi cites the ל of משתאה לה as parallel to the ל in אמרי לי and לאשתו, illustrating that ל here means 'concerning / at', not 'to'."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:00:17.731769094Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}